  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello. What brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S022",
      "patient_response": "Well, hello, doctor. I guess... I mean, my supervisor said I needed to come in and get checked out, but if you want to know what actually brings me in, it goes back to our staff meeting on Tuesday. We always have these mandatory meetings in the big break room down at the facility where I work—I'm a personal care aide, you see, so we deal with a lot of older folks. And fall prevention is just... it's huge. It's the most important thing we do, because if somebody goes down, it's a whole mess of paperwork and ambulances and it's just bad. \n\nAnyway, we were all sitting there, and the manager was talking about protocols, and I just got so... I don't know, worked up? Like, the energy was just buzzing right through my chest and I felt like they weren't explaining it right or taking it seriously enough. So I just stood up. I had to show them how it actually happens on the floor. I started acting out this whole fall-prevention drill right there in the middle of the room. I grabbed a couple of the folding chairs—the heavy grey metal ones—and I was dragging them around to show the angles of the beds, and my arms were just waving everywhere, demonstrating how you have to hook under their arms and block their momentum with your knee. \n\nAnd the thing is... I just kept going. I couldn't seem to stop. My supervisor, Brenda, she was saying, \"Okay, we get it, please sit down,\" and a couple of the other folks were telling me to sit down and take a breath, but I just couldn't. It was like my brain and my body just had to finish the whole routine, moving the chairs and waving around, even though everyone was just staring at me like I was crazy. I just couldn't turn the motor off, you know? So... yeah. That's why they told me I had to come see you.",
